1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,167
In post-war Japan,

2
00:00:01,167 --> 00:00:03,696
Toyota engineer Taiichi Ohno gave every

3
00:00:03,696 --> 00:00:06,354
frontline factory worker the authority to

4
00:00:06,354 --> 00:00:08,494
freeze an entire assembly line by

5
00:00:08,494 --> 00:00:10,180
pulling one overhead rope.

6
00:00:10,180 --> 00:00:13,200
Old mass-production plants forbade line stops,

7
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:15,827
pushing crooked doors and stripped bolts

8
00:00:15,827 --> 00:00:17,993
into giant repair yards out back.

9
00:00:17,993 --> 00:00:20,094
Ohno's Andon cord paused work at

10
00:00:20,094 --> 00:00:21,539
the very first defect,

11
00:00:21,539 --> 00:00:23,771
trading a two-minute source fix to

12
00:00:23,771 --> 00:00:26,660
prevent thousands of flawed cars downstream.

13
00:00:26,660 --> 00:00:29,505
Solo operators repeat Detroit's mistake whenever

14
00:00:29,505 --> 00:00:30,927
they wire up AI scripts,

15
00:00:30,927 --> 00:00:31,460
paid ads,

16
00:00:31,460 --> 00:00:34,127
or outreach pipelines without a stop-trigger,

17
00:00:34,127 --> 00:00:36,972
multiplying embarrassing errors at machine speed

18
00:00:36,972 --> 00:00:38,750
while chasing non-stop volume.

19
00:00:38,750 --> 00:00:40,962
Install a hard Andon cord inside

20
00:00:40,962 --> 00:00:42,552
every automated system:

21
00:00:42,552 --> 00:00:44,764
set strict daily API spend caps,

22
00:00:44,764 --> 00:00:45,732
schema checks,

23
00:00:45,732 --> 00:00:48,359
and alert triggers that halt execution

24
00:00:48,359 --> 00:00:49,880
on anomaly number one.

25
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:52,011
Pause the line for two minutes

26
00:00:52,011 --> 00:00:53,786
at the very first defect,

27
00:00:53,786 --> 00:00:56,698
because fixing root causes early protects

28
00:00:56,698 --> 00:00:58,900
years of margin and reputation.
